\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Registration form enforcing field completeness. The form cannot be submitted until all required fields are populated, preventing incomplete account creation.}}{42}{figure.caption.62}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces Email verification step requiring entry of a six-digit code sent to the clinician's registered address, confirming ownership before account activation.}}{42}{figure.caption.63}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Recovery code generation screen presented at account activation. The clinician must copy or download the codes as a text file before proceeding, establishing a secure fallback authentication method.}}{43}{figure.caption.64}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces Multi-factor authentication method selection screen, offering the clinician a choice between a TOTP authenticator application and a recovery authentication code.}}{43}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces Password reset interface allowing clinicians to securely regain access to their account through a guided credential recovery flow.}}{44}{figure.caption.66}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces Patients Dashboard showing the master-detail layout. The left sidebar presents the patient navigator with search, filter, and episode management controls. The main panel displays the full clinical summary for the selected patient, including active conditions, medications, recent laboratory results, procedures, and a scoped encounter history.}}{45}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.14}{\ignorespaces Patient access grant modal presenting the six-cell OTP input field. The clinician enters the code generated on the patient's device to initiate the access request handshake.}}{46}{figure.caption.73}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.15}{\ignorespaces Categorical Patient Journey view representing medical events as a hierarchical left-to-right graph. High-priority events display animated priority rings, sibling relationships are labelled on each node, and the detail sidebar is visible on the right.}}{52}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.16}{\ignorespaces Chronological Patient Journey view displaying medical events along a horizontal D3.js timeline with pan and zoom. Parallel sub-branches represent alternative diagnostic hypotheses, each on its own horizontal track. The completed branch is merged back into the main timeline via a green arc, and the discontinued branch is blocked with a red wall marker. A ``CASE CLOSED'' flag is rendered at the timeline terminus when the main timeline is set to the Finished state.}}{54}{figure.caption.93}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.17}{\ignorespaces Zoomed view of a completed branch in the chronological timeline. The green curved merge arc connects the branch's final node back to the main timeline, with a ``MERGED'' label rendered above the arc. The branch line is rendered in green to indicate the validated state.}}{55}{figure.caption.94}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.18}{\ignorespaces Zoomed view of a discontinued branch in the chronological timeline. A pulsing red wall marker is rendered at the branch terminus with a ``DISCONTINUED'' label. The branch line is rendered in red, and all add, edit, and delete operations on nodes within this branch are blocked.}}{55}{figure.caption.95}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.19}{\ignorespaces Tutorial Step 4 of 18: Expand a branch. The categorical view shows first-level nodes in their collapsed default state. The \texttt {+} button on the CBC Blood Test node is highlighted with a pulse animation, and the tutorial card instructs the clinician to click it to expand the sub-tree. The step advances automatically upon interaction.}}{57}{figure.caption.96}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.20}{\ignorespaces Tutorial Step 10 of 18: Switch to Chronological view. The view toggle in the header is highlighted, and the tutorial card instructs the clinician to click ``Chronological'' to transition the layout from the hierarchical tree to the D3.js timeline. The step advances automatically once the toggle is activated.}}{57}{figure.caption.97}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.21}{\ignorespaces Tutorial Step 16 of 18: Main timeline state. The chronological view is shown with the branch state menu open on the main timeline line, presenting the In Progress and Finish Case options. The tutorial card instructs the clinician to click the main timeline to open this menu, advancing automatically once the interaction occurs.}}{58}{figure.caption.98}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.22}{\ignorespaces AI-assisted clinical chat in its compact panel view, rendered alongside the Patient Journey Dashboard. The session list, message history, response mode selector, and composer are all visible within the panel.}}{60}{figure.caption.104}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.23}{\ignorespaces AI-assisted clinical chat in maximised mode, expanding to fill the full viewport. This mode is intended for extended clinical reasoning sessions and is also the default view for conversations opened via the Fork action.}}{60}{figure.caption.105}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.24}{\ignorespaces Doctor Profile page showing the practitioner information panel on the left and the Schedule Manager on the right, with slot creation form, status filters, and a today's summary sidebar.}}{62}{figure.caption.108}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.25}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram illustrating the full MedGraph interaction lifecycle: iframe initialisation, postMessage-based node operations, backend confirmation, optimistic rollback on failure, and stateful mode switching.}}{64}{figure.caption.109}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.26}{\ignorespaces Component diagram illustrating the architecture of the dashboard, MedGraph iframe boundary, AI chat module, application services, and backend integration points.}}{65}{figure.caption.110}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.27}{\ignorespaces Data flow diagram for a MedGraph node CRUD operation. The flow begins with a user interaction in the graph engine (iframe), passes through the postMessage boundary into the Angular host, is forwarded to the FHIR backend, and returns via a confirmation or error path. On success, the temporary node ID is replaced with the persistent UUID. On failure, the pending operation is rolled back from the corresponding state map.}}{66}{figure.caption.111}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.28}{\ignorespaces CFI-Care Graph Logic Test Suite dashboard after a full run. All 62 tests across five suites pass with zero failures and zero warnings in 22ms total. The scoreboard, per-suite result panels, performance bar chart, and log console are visible.}}{69}{figure.caption.115}%
